WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Congratulations. You made it. You're about to start the 7th class of 7 in

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.000
Concise, Clear and Confident
Communication. If for some reason you

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.840
found this last video first then you can
go back and do the whole course.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.070
Otherwise if you've been going all the
way through, I'm really happy for you.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:20.730
I encourage you to watch these videos as
many times as you need to to really make

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:24.779
these tips and strategies habit. Because
that's where it's going to start to make

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:25.980
a difference in your life.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.480
So let's wrap things up.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.380
In our previous
lesson we looked at how to get rid of

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:40.880
fillers. In lesson 7, we're going to hit
on confident nonverbals. So tip 7 is

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:47.100
confident nonverbals enhance your message. All the previous six lessons are mostly

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.129
to do with words and how you sound

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:56.169
verbally. But, these nonverbal messages
can enhance or detract from what you're saying.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.370
So people form impressions about how you come across through nonverbal

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:04.049
communication. Distracting nonverbals
will diminish or take away from your

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:08.729
communication, your message and confident nonverbals will add to it. To say

00:01:08.729 --> 00:01:14.100
something "with confidence" means that you sound and look confident not just that

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.049
you're saying confident words.

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:20.520
Strategy number one. Use direct eye
contact.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:27.180
Most people drastically underestimate
the power of direct eye contact. Make

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.439
direct eye contact when speaking and
listening. Both are very important. Strive

00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:35.640
for 80% of the time in your
conversations and 99% of

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:41.549
the time during presentations. Most of us
underestimate the need that others have

00:01:41.549 --> 00:01:45.360
for us to be looking directly at them.
When we speak and so those numbers will

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:51.630
give you a good gauge. Adrian Furnham a
PhD researcher that's spent a lot of

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.860
time working on eye contact said "The
confident, the bright, and the socially

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.390
dominant, look more with direct eye
contact, while it is the opposite for the

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:05.880
socially anxious. In this society we
generally interpret direct eye contact

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:11.819
as confident and the lack of direct eye
contact as signaling some kind of issue.

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:12.880
Often times

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.520
social anxiety or even just a lack of
confidence in general. "Direct" means right

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:23.350
into their eyes. Not like these two people
who are looking elsewhere. Sometimes

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:27.640
above people's head sometimes over their shoulders. You do not want to be in this

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:32.800
kind of situation. When you're looking
directly at somebody, it says, hey, I see you.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.500
And they look back and it's, hey,  I see
you too.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.120
There's a connection there. So what does
that look like in practice? Like you're

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:44.020
looking directly into the lens of the
camera. Someone says hey look right here.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.530
We're going to take a picture. You want
to look right into their eyes. And again

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:51.640
in a conversation, it's 80% of
the time. You don't want you to stare,

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:53.320
but you want to look at them

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:57.280
the vast majority of the time to show
them that you're communicating. You're

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.850
locked into what you're saying and
you're locked into listening to what

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:07.810
they say. Strategy two is to avoid "up
talk." This is a nonverbal tendency.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:12.160
Up talk describes the upward inflection
used to make a statement sound like a

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:18.250
question, sometimes called up speak. And it sounds like this. I wanted to be to

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:23.470
discuss the plans? I was hoping to have
that nail down today? Many of the new

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:28.330
hires need this training badly? Now
clearly these are not questions but

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:33.520
you've probably heard up speak or up
talk from people. Maybe you do it. It's

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.990
becoming much more common among the younger generation and it's become a

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:43.180
real issue in professional settings. But
instead of this, you want your sentences to

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:49.180
go down at the end. So if there's a period, you want them to go down like the period signals.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:53.650
I wanted to meet to discuss the plans. I
was hoping to have the nail down today.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:57.459
Many of the new hires need this training
badly.

00:03:57.459 --> 00:04:02.260
That's a statement. And that's what it
should sound like. You may not realize,

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:06.160
however, that you're up talking. A lot of
people don't even know they're doing it.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.770
I recommend asking a trusted friend to
listen to you talk to see if you do it.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:16.450
And then commit. If your are doing up
talk, I want you to commit to landing at

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:21.549
the end of sentences. And boy is this a
hard habit to break. But it's well worth

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:24.310
it because you don't want to sound like
the person who's never

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:31.000
sure of what they're saying. Strategy 3
is to smile. Who doesn't like a smile?

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.169
People often don't realize that they're
scowling or have that stone-faced

00:04:35.169 --> 00:04:38.950
expression when they're walking around
talking to people but many people just

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:43.180
don't smile. And, as a result, they don't
sound confident and they don't look

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:46.690
confident to other people. Even if their
words are confident they're not going to

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:49.600
come across as feeling really
comfortable with what they say. Because

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.850
confident people smile. So you want to
practice smiling a little as you talk. I

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:59.770
don't think you should plaster on a
phony smile that's just not me. But you

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:03.430
want to practice smiling a little. So in
other words instead of a stone-faced for

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:08.200
that scowl, be aware of your facial
expression and if you're happy then show

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:09.880
it with your face.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:13.840
Sarah Silverman, a comedian, was talking
to a young friend of hers who had that

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.340
scowl on her face even though she claims
she wasn't upset. And Sarah Silverman

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:22.450
said, "I going to change your life right now. If that's your default face, put a smile

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:27.850
on there." A lot of us need to warm up our
facial expression so we come across more

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:33.789
confident in what we're saying. So use
confident nonverbals. Our takeaway

00:05:33.789 --> 00:05:38.110
strategies from this lesson are to use
direct eye contact. That is number one.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:43.000
Avoid up talk. And if you do this, you
want to ask a friend to listen and give

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:46.720
you that give you that advice and then
come down at the end your sentences. And

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.370
then practice smiling a little. Warm that
face up and communicate that confidence.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:57.789
So practice these tips and strategies. As
Jim Rohn said, "Motivation is what get

00:05:57.789 --> 00:06:03.070
you started. Habit is what keeps you
going." Speaking of nice concise statements,

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:07.930
that's an amazing one and it couldn't be
more true. So you're learning some things by

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:12.700
watching these lessons once. I recommend you watch them as many times as you need to

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:19.630
to put these tips into practice and
make them a habit. It's been a pleasure

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:24.190
learning with you through this
mini-course on Concise, Clear, and

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:27.550
Confident Communication. I encourage you to put them into practice and I look

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.330
forward to learning with you in the
future.

